      "timestamp_raw": "Jul 8, 2012 0:38:37 GMT -5",
      "body_html": "S. rosea is rather variable from one population to the next.  Even within a single site, the genetic diversity, while subtle, is rather extensive.  <br/><br/>Here's a population of S. rosea, with the standard \"tubby\" looking pitchers:<br/><br/><img src=\"http://i1110.photobucket.com/albums/h443/meizzwang/IMG_8225.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%;\"/><br/><br/><img src=\"http://i1110.photobucket.com/albums/h443/meizzwang/IMG_8226.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%;\"/><br/><br/><img src=\"http://i1110.photobucket.com/albums/h443/meizzwang/IMG_8310.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%;\"/><br/><br/><img src=\"http://i1110.photobucket.com/albums/h443/meizzwang/IMG_8309.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%;\"/><br/><br/><img src=\"http://i1110.photobucket.com/albums/h443/meizzwang/IMG_8308.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%;\"/><br/><br/><img src=\"http://i1110.photobucket.com/albums/h443/meizzwang/IMG_8307.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%;\"/><br/><br/><img src=\"http://i1110.photobucket.com/albums/h443/meizzwang/IMG_8306.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%;\"/>",
